---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { config } from '../../lib/config';

// Privacy policy template, pending legal review. Keep the escape-hatch list
// in sync with tokenpak/telemetry/{config,request_logger}.py and the
// TOKENPAK_DEBUG CLI surface, and keep the optional network requests in sync
// with tokenpak/core/_update_check.py, tokenpak/telemetry/{reporter,install_reporter}.py
// and tokenpak/compression/fingerprinting/sync.py.
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — TokenPak"
  description="How TokenPak handles data on your machine. Local-first by default."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-prose-wide px-6 py-12 prose">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-tp-rule bg-tp-paper text-tp-ink p-4 mb-8 text-small">
      <strong>Plain-English data-handling summary.</strong> This page
      describes what TokenPak does on your machine today. It is not a substitute
      for legal advice and has not been reviewed by counsel. TokenPak is a local
      proxy with no cloud component, so the disclosures here describe local file
      paths, opt-in controls and the optional network requests you can turn on
      — not a service operated on your behalf.
    </div>

    <h1>Privacy</h1>

    <p class="text-tp-mute">Last updated: 2026-10-06 (draft).</p>

    <h2>What runs where</h2>
    <p>
      TokenPak is a <strong>local proxy</strong>. Every compression, routing,
      caching, and telemetry decision happens on your machine. Your prompts,
      completions, code, and business data are <em>not</em> sent to any
      TokenPak-operated service: none sits in your request path. By default, the
      one thing that leaves your machine is the LLM request you ask TokenPak to
      proxy: it travels to the upstream provider you configure, using your own
      credentials (see <em>What leaves your machine</em> below).
    </p>

    <h2>What we collect by default</h2>
    <p>
      By default, nothing: zero telemetry, no usage pings, no install counters.
      The opt-in debug and logging controls described below can expand what is
      stored <em>locally on your machine</em> when you enable them — they write
      only to local destinations (file, stdout, or your system's local syslog
      socket) and do not initiate any outbound network connection for logging
      purposes. The few optional network requests TokenPak can make are listed
      under <em>What leaves your machine</em>.
    </p>

    <h2>What TokenPak stores locally on your machine</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A SQLite ledger at <code>~/.tokenpak/monitor.db</code> (or <code>~/.tpk/monitor.db</code> on fresh installs) with per-request metadata (model, token counts, latency, cost, cache-origin). Default retention: 90 days of rolling history.</li>
      <li>Configuration at <code>~/.tokenpak/config.yaml</code> (or <code>~/.tpk/config.yaml</code> on fresh installs). Contains profile selection, port, compression knobs. Never contains API keys or prompts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Optional debug and logging controls</h2>
    <p>
      Several opt-in controls <strong>will</strong> expand what is stored locally if
      you turn them on. They are off by default. We disclose them here so you
      know exactly what each knob does before enabling it.
    </p>
    <dl class="space-y-4">
      <div>
        <dt><code>TOKENPAK_DEBUG=1</code> (env var)</dt>
        <dd>Enables verbose debug output on stdout/stderr and debug-level entries in local logs. Debug output <em>may</em> include request headers (never credential values — those are redacted at the logging boundary). Off by default.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><code>TOKENPAK_LOG_ENABLED=1</code> + <code>TOKENPAK_LOG_DESTINATION</code> (env vars)</dt>
        <dd>Enables the structured request logger. Destinations: <code>file</code> (default path), <code>stdout</code>, <code>syslog</code>. Logger records request metadata — not prompt/response bodies unless <code>store_prompts</code> is also enabled. See <code>TOKENPAK_LOG_LEVEL</code> and <code>TOKENPAK_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS</code> for tuning.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><code>telemetry.store_prompts = true</code> (config flag)</dt>
        <dd><strong>This flag stores prompt and response bodies to local disk.</strong> Off by default. Exists for debugging and benchmarking. When enabled, prompt/response content is written to the same retention store as metadata. Clients with regulated data should leave this off.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2>What leaves your machine</h2>
    <p>By default, only what you ask TokenPak to proxy:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>LLM requests</strong> — forwarded to the provider you configured (Anthropic, OpenAI, etc.), using <em>your</em> credentials on the way out. TokenPak forwards the request to that provider; it does not send a copy to any TokenPak-operated service.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The requests below happen only if you opt in or run the command yourself.
      None of them sends your prompts, responses, file contents or credentials.
    </p>
    <dl class="space-y-4">
      <div>
        <dt>Update check (asks first)</dt>
        <dd>TokenPak asks, in an interactive terminal, whether it may check for new releases. The default answer is no, and nothing is requested until you say yes. If you agree, it reads <code>https://pypi.org/pypi/tokenpak/json</code> at most once a day. The request carries no TokenPak project, prompt, completion, usage, credential or file data, but PyPI sees ordinary connection metadata such as your IP address. Turn it off with <code>tokenpak update --disable-checks</code> or <code>TOKENPAK_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code>. Running <code>tokenpak update --check</code> yourself makes a one-time request to the same address.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Usage metrics (<code>TOKENPAK_METRICS_ENABLED=1</code>)</dt>
        <dd>Off by default. If you turn it on, TokenPak can send content-free usage metrics to TokenPak-operated endpoints (<code>metrics.tokenpak.ai</code> and <code>api.tokenpak.ai</code>). The data is limited to the date and time of the report, token counts, model names, compression ratio, latency, the profile and invocation mode in use, the TokenPak version, your operating system and Python version, a random install ID and a 24-hour request count. It never includes prompts, responses, file names or API keys.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><code>tokenpak fingerprint sync</code> (a command you run)</dt>
        <dd>Sends a structural fingerprint of the text you give it (segment types, token estimates and counts, not the text itself) to <code>intelligence.tokenpak.ai</code> and receives suggested directives back. Add <code>--dry-run</code> to see exactly what would be sent.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2>Third parties</h2>
    <p>
      TokenPak OSS has no sub-processors, and no TokenPak-operated service sits
      in your request path. Your prompts, completions, and credentials never
      reach TokenPak. The only network destination for the requests you proxy is
      the upstream LLM provider you configure (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, etc.),
      reached using your own credentials. The optional requests listed under
      <em>What leaves your machine</em> are separate from your proxied requests.
    </p>

    <h2>Your controls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>tokenpak uninstall --soft</code> restores your Claude Code settings, reverses the Codex companion install and stops a running proxy. It keeps your configuration and local data.</li>
      <li><code>tokenpak uninstall --hard</code> also deletes TokenPak's local files, such as configuration, request databases and caches. It keeps your session journal, budget history and saved capsules; delete those yourself if you want them gone. Add <code>--dry-run</code> to either command to see exactly what it would change.</li>
      <li>Clients you configured with <code>tokenpak integrate &lt;client&gt; --apply</code> can be restored with <code>tokenpak integrate &lt;client&gt; --revert</code>.</li>
      <li>Unset any optional escape-hatch env var to disable that source of data collection.</li>
      <li>Uninstalling the package (<code>pip uninstall tokenpak</code>) stops all TokenPak execution on your machine.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions: <a href="mailto:hello@tokenpak.ai">hello@tokenpak.ai</a>.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
